---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/eng-design-fab/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Session 2: Materials for Subtractive Manufacturing</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">Unit 2 &mdash; Manufacturing Foundations, Week 1</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Session 2</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Materials for Subtractive Manufacturing</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Last Time: Who You're Building For</h2>
              <p>Today: what you're actually going to build it out of.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Before a single toolpath gets programmed, material choice is
                already shaping what's possible.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Not All Sheet Goods Are the Same</h2>
              <p>Three common CNC-router materials, three very different personalities.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Three Options</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Baltic birch plywood</b> &mdash; many thin plies, minimal voids</li>
                <li class="fragment"><b>Standard plywood</b> &mdash; fewer, thicker plies, cheaper, less consistent</li>
                <li class="fragment"><b>MDF</b> &mdash; no grain at all, engineered from wood fiber</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Grain Direction &amp; Tear-Out</h2>
              <p>
                Plywood has a grain. Cutting across it, instead of with it,
                is far more likely to chip and tear the edge.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                MDF has no grain at all &mdash; it cuts consistently in every
                direction, which is exactly why it's a CNC favorite despite
                its other weaknesses.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Screw-Holding</h2>
              <p>
                Not all materials hold a screw the same way &mdash; and an
                arcade cabinet gets opened for service, not just built once.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Screw-Holding</h2>
              <ul>
                <li class="fragment">Plywood holds screws reasonably well, especially face-on</li>
                <li class="fragment">MDF holds a screw the <i>first</i> time &mdash; strip it once and the hole is often ruined</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Weight, Cost, Moisture</h2>
              <p>
                MDF is denser (heavier) and cheaper than either plywood.
                None of these three are meant for outdoor moisture exposure
                &mdash; but they don't all fail the same way or the same speed.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>The Property Comparison</h2>
              <table class="table table-dark table-sm">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th></th><th>Baltic Birch</th><th>Standard Plywood</th><th>MDF</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Edge quality when routed</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Fair</td><td>Excellent</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Screw-holding</td><td>Good</td><td>Good</td><td>Poor (edge), fine (face, once)</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Weight</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Heavy</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Cost</td><td>Highest</td><td>Middle</td><td>Lowest</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Your Cabinet Isn't One Material</h2>
              <p>
                A side panel, a control panel, and a back access panel don't
                have the same demands &mdash; there's no reason they should
                use the same material by default.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">"Cheapest" isn't the same question as "best."</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                A material choice should point back to a specific property
                that specific part actually needs.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Things to Remember</h2>
              <p><b class="text-sn-theme">A part that gets serviced needs to survive being unscrewed.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                This is exactly why screw-holding matters more for some
                panels than others.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Time</h2>
              <p>Session 3: Additive Manufacturing</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                A completely different process, with its own material rules
                &mdash; and its own place in this project.
              </p>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3>Step 1: The Property Comparison</h3>
          <p>Use this table for your reasoning below &mdash; don't just go with a gut feeling.</p>
          <table class="table table-dark table-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr><th></th><th>Baltic Birch</th><th>Standard Plywood</th><th>MDF</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Edge quality when routed</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Fair</td><td>Excellent</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Screw-holding</td><td>Good</td><td>Good</td><td>Poor (edge), fine (face, once)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Weight</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Heavy</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Cost</td><td>Highest</td><td>Middle</td><td>Lowest</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 2: Choose a Material for Three Components</h3>
          <p>
            For each component below, choose Baltic birch, standard plywood,
            or MDF, and justify it using <b class="text-sn-theme">at least
            two specific properties</b> from the table &mdash; not just cost.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><b>Side panel</b> &mdash; structural, holds the cabinet's shape and any leg levelers/casters</li>
            <li><b>Control panel</b> &mdash; where joysticks and buttons mount, takes repeated physical impact</li>
            <li><b>Back access panel</b> &mdash; removed and reattached whenever the electronics need servicing</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Step 3: Defend Your Weakest Choice</h3>
          <p>
            Of your three choices, which one are you least sure about?
            Write one sentence on what would change your mind.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you move on, check your work against these two questions.</p>

          <div class="text-start">
            <dl>
              <dt>Did you use at least two properties per choice?</dt>
              <dd>
                "It's cheaper" alone isn't a complete justification for any of
                the three components.
              </dd>
              <dt>Would this make sense to a client?</dt>
              <dd>
                Your reasoning should hold up even to someone who's never
                heard the word "MDF" before today.
              </dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these on your own. Reflections are individual, even when the rest of the session was team work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>A classmate picks MDF for a bracket with a bolt threaded into its <b>edge</b>, saying &ldquo;MDF is the cheapest and its routed edge quality is excellent.&rdquo; Both of those facts are true. Why is the choice still wrong?</li>
            <li>Session 3 covers additive manufacturing. Is there any component of an arcade cabinet you'd consider 3D printing instead of routing from sheet material? Why?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit your three material choices with justifications, your weakest-choice defense, and your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
